A||||7||ordovician||what the hell could this possibly be?||||4||
Z||000000||ordovician||12-13-2001||08:20 PM||||ok. first of all, hello to all you fellow fiero owners. it has come as quite a shock to find this place.<P>ok, now for the hard stuff. i am told a few things about this problem, first that my clutch is about to die, second that my hydraulic system is about to die. this is what it does;<BR>when i take it out in the morning, it will not go into gear, unless i pump the clutch a shitload of times. throughout the course of the day, i have to pump it a few times to keep it shifting smooth. if i dont pump the clutch, the shifter will either be stuck in neutral, or stuck in gear and not move at all. what do you think?||||reg||4
Z||000001||Old Lar||12-13-2001||08:32 PM||||I think you need some transmission work.  If you are not able or willing to do the work yourself, maybe someone knows some good mechanics in Ct.  Bob Parker at Kick Hill Farms in Lebanon Ct has many parts and I think does some repairs.  The transmission fluid may be low, the shift cables may be bad, the linkage may need adjustment.<P>If you are into repairs, buy the HAYNES Manual for the Fiero for some trouble shooting  and diagnostic guidence.||||reg||1
Z||000002||avengador1||12-13-2001||08:32 PM||||Sounds like you have a leak and air is getting into the system. Might be time for a new master or slave cylinder. Use genuine GM parts as they seem to be the best. Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||REDHOTT88||12-13-2001||08:36 PM||||Welcome to the Forum!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I stumbled onto this place a while back after I bought my first Fiero.<P>I'm not a tranny expert, so if no one responds, or while you are waiting, try clicking on "search " in the upper right hand corner. Type in a few keywords and you'll find lots of previous answers to the same question you asked.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR><B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (Red and shiny all over, runs smooth, shifts crisp!)</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||12-13-2001||09:17 PM||||&lt;spooky music&gt;<P>You are entering a strange place, a world of mystery and imagination.  You are entering . . . <I>The Hydraulic Zone</I>!<P><BR>It sounds like your car has clutch hydraulic problems, probably due to leaking master and/or slave cylinder.  If you search past posts -- or just hang around here a while -- you will find lots of info on the subject . . . maybe more than you want to know.<P><BR><I>Edit</I><P>p.s.  Welcome to the forum!  I'm Precambrian, myself.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by MrPBody (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||GTDude||12-14-2001||06:34 PM||||Definitely sounds like a hydralic problem to me.  Seen same thing happen in Sunbirds and S10's.  It's usually comes from sucking air somewhere, but can be just a bad master cyl. or slave cyl.  Either way I'd replace them both....not sure but they may come as a unit.Good luck<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||Zack Doyle||12-14-2001||06:57 PM||||check the differential fluid.  I had something similar to those symptoms and I went all out and spent too much money.  My dad figured it out for me.||||reg||8
Z||000007||Jncomutt||12-14-2001||07:23 PM||||Same thing happened to me when I first got my car.  Turned out to be the slave cyl.  <P>And welcome to the forum!!  Woo Hoo, another CT member, if ya ever need anything just let me know..<P>John||||reg||1
